# Target C — bridge-independence correction for the `mu_4` train-pairing endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Target:** C-II  
**Classification:** proved candidate; unpromoted correction audit

## Purpose

The conditional endpoint note split off a possible linear bridge corridor because it
did not identify which outer-walk edges can actually be train endpoints. The exact
rhombic-complex boundary classification removes that caveat:

```text
an outer-face bridge has no bounded rhombus on either side
 -> it is not a train endpoint
 -> the bridge corridor can only delete train endpoints
 -> the mu_4 train-pairing area contradiction is unconditional.
```

This note does not claim C-II closure. It excludes only the Section-96 equality
endpoint; wrapping cells with larger normalized diameter and the macroscopic
inactive-container branch remain.

## 1. Exact boundary classification

Let `H_D` be the induced carrier graph and let `mathcal R_D` be all its bounded
strict-rhombus faces. A train endpoint is an edge occurrence incident to exactly one
face of `mathcal R_D`.

For every such occurrence, the other side is exactly one of:

1. the outer face of `H_D`, in which case the edge occurrence belongs to one of the
   outer attachment subwalks `W_{i,D}`; or
2. a bounded nonquadrilateral face of `H_D`.

There is no third case. In particular, a bridge of `H_D` has the outer face on both
sides and is incident to no bounded rhombus, so it is not a train endpoint. The total
number of endpoints of type 2 is at most

\[
 \sum_{f\text{ nonquadrilateral}}\deg(f)\le5Q_D=o(D)
\]

at the Section-96 endpoint. This conclusion does not assume `b_D=o(D)` and does not
replace the outer walk by the rhombic boundary count.

## 2. Endpoint support and route data

Use the Section-96 equality and endpoint support saturation. With

\[
 \theta_4=2\arcsin\mu_4,
 \quad
 \phi_4=2\pi-2\theta_4,
 \quad
 \alpha_4=3\theta_4-2\pi={3\over2}\sin\theta_4,
\]

one has

\[
 a_D/D\to\alpha_4,
 \quad
 Q_D+s_D+c_D=o(D),
 \quad
 \operatorname{area}(P_D)/D^2\to
 A_4={\pi\over4}-{3\over8}(\phi_4-\sin\phi_4).
\]

The three forbidden-gap routes have total length `3 sin(theta_4)D+o(D)`, each
has `o(D)` excess over its chord, and all but `o(D)` ordinary route edges lie on
two-edge attachment subwalks. The ordinary route edge count is therefore at most
`2 alpha_4 D+o(D)`.

## 3. Distinct long-route directions

The three long-route chord directions are pairwise distinct modulo `pi`. If two
forbidden arcs of length `phi_4` had antipodal midpoints, choose one complementary
allowed arc `J`, of length `pi-phi_4`. The values `x in J` for which either `x` or
`x+pi` lies in the third forbidden arc are the intersections with two antipodal
intervals of length `phi_4`. Their centers are `pi` apart. Since

\[
 (\pi-\phi_4)+\phi_4=\pi,
 \qquad \phi_4<\pi/2,
\]

at most one of those two intervals can meet the interior of `J` (simultaneous
intersection can occur only at the two endpoint tangencies). Thus some `x` and
`x+pi` both remain in the allowed support, contradicting limiting diameter
`mu_4<1`. Hence the long-route directions are distinct.

The geodesic-excess identity makes all but `o(D)` edges of each long route arbitrarily
close in direction to its chord. Endpoint-density equality also makes all but `o(D)`
ordinary gaps have angular size `(2+o(1))/D`; hence their two-edge routes have the
fixed equilateral local offsets from the circle tangent. The saturated attachment
density then makes only `o(D)` ordinary two-edge route edges parallel to any fixed
long-route direction: a direction window of width `eta` contains `O(eta D)+o(D)`
ordinary edges, and then `eta` tends to zero.

## 4. Train-pairing span bound without a bridge assumption

Discard the `o(D)` train endpoints facing nonquadrilateral faces. Every remaining train
endpoint is on an actual outer attachment subwalk; bridge occurrences simply do not
appear among them.

Train edges are parallel within one train. Therefore, after discarding `o(D)` edge
outliers:

* a long-route endpoint cannot pair with an endpoint on a different long route;
* only `o(D)` long-route endpoints can pair with ordinary-route endpoints; and
* endpoints on one long route pair only within that route.

The last class has total transverse span `o(D^2)`, because each long route has
`o(D)` Hausdorff distance from its chord. The only trains with potentially macroscopic
span therefore have two ordinary-route endpoints. There are at most

\[
 \alpha_4D+o(D)
\]

of them, since the ordinary routes supply at most `2 alpha_4D+o(D)` endpoint
occurrences. This estimate is an upper bound even if a linear bridge corridor removes
some ordinary or long outer-walk edges.

For every train, its transverse span is at most `diam(P_D)`, and summing spans counts
each rhombus twice. Since the rhombic area is `area(P_D)-o(D^2)`,

\[
 2A_4\le\alpha_4\mu_4.
\]

The two sides are

\[
 2A_4=1.236749759657516\ldots,
 \qquad
 \alpha_4\mu_4=0.924074023352554\ldots,
\]

which is impossible.

## 5. Consequence

The Section-96 endpoint `diam(P_D)/D\to\mu_4` is excluded without a bridge-free
assumption. Compactness therefore gives a qualitative positive gap above `mu_4` for
wrapping-cell sequences satisfying the canonical endpoint hypotheses.

This is not the full C-II closure: the gap is not quantified, the larger-diameter
wrapping regime is not excluded, and the macroscopic inactive-container branch is
untouched.

